on.\n\nFor Plan-002, I trained a small language model on the labeled data with a standard supervised setup, and the results show the model reaches 58% accuracy on the validation set, which beats the majority baseline but reveals a clear weakness: the deep-internal class is being systematically confused with fast-internal, suggesting the model struggles to distinguish between these two modalities even though they should have different behavioral signatures. The training converged but the loss plateaued higher than ideal, and there's mild overfitting creeping in after the second epoch—though I ran short on GPU hours due to server issues.\n\nNow I'm looking at the performance metrics for different routing strategies across Plan-003, comparing simpler baselines like always-internal and always-deterministic against more sophisticated approaches like confidence thresholding and the cited CogRouter system, and I'm seeing that while my simgate approach gets reasonable gains over the internal routing baseline, the deterministic routing improvement isn't statistically significant given the confidence intervals.\n\nLooking at the failure modes, the catastrophic failure rate drops substantially when using simgate compared to pure internal routing, and I'm tracking which task types show the most benefit from this approach. For Plan-004, I'm comparing explicit routing decisions against an implicit backbone approach and finding no meaningful difference between them despite the explicit method winning on slightly more tasks. The heatmap analysis for Plan-005 shows that only the high-risk cluster stands out visually, with no clear pattern across different phases of execution.\n\nNow examining the router behavior more closely—most tasks show high entropy in routing decisions, suggesting the router is operating near-randomly on the majority of them. Plan-006 tests whether adding more routing options helps, but the 2-way versus 3-way comparison shows negligible differences. Plan-007 re