ways-internal as a sanity check.\n\nNow I'm structuring the results tables for the different plans — a main results table showing performance across risk tiers, ablation tables with confidence intervals, a routing heatmap, and tables for router size comparisons. Let me finalize this in YAML format.\n\nFor each plan's rationale, I need to explain why these performance patterns emerge under worst-tier assumptions — specifically, when the backbone model is competent enough that internal reasoning rarely fails catastrophically except on tasks involving complex physical state transitions where hallucinated states lead to agent death, while simpler task logic like object finding and classification produces similar outcomes across all three modalities.\n\nThe 42% ambiguous labels from plan-001 means the router learns from a noisy signal, struggling especially with the deep-internal class since it only clearly helps on a handful of task types where extended reasoning prevents specific error modes. The router essentially picks up a crude binary pattern — routing to deterministic on death-risk tasks, internal otherwise — but this signal is weak because death risk depends on state, not task type, resulting in 58% accuracy that's only modestly above the 42% majority baseline.\n\nNow I'm working through the SimGate rationale at 65.2...\n\nthe Qwen2.5-7B backbone has fundamental limitations on complex tasks regardless of routing choice. The 0.4-point gap between SimGate and always-deterministic (with confidence intervals crossing zero) suggests selective routing doesn't meaningfully outperform just always using deterministic. This happens because the router's 58% accuracy causes it to miss many steps where deterministic would help, while the penalty for routing to deterministic on safe tasks is minimal since deterministic only underperforms by 5-8 points there. The asymmetric cost structure — routing to internal on death-risk tasks carries a -100 penalty but routing to deterministic on safe tasks costs little — makes always-deterministic surprisingly competitive.\n\nFor plan-004, explicit and implicit routing converge because the underlying routing signal is too weak to distinguish between them, with noisy labels and no clear structural patterns to exploit. the underlying signal is weak, the architectural choice of how to learn it matters less. The 7B backbone has more capacity to detect routing-relevant features in context but is less efficient; the 1.7B router is more focused but has less capacity. These trade-offs roughly cancel, producing an insignificant 1.2-point gap. This is the worst-case outcome for C2: the explicit modular router provides no measurable advantage over implicit routing, undermining the core architectural claim.\n\nLooking at the routing patterns, death-risk tasks form a visually distinct cluster with >65% deterministic routing, but the remaining 11 task types scatter between 20-45% with no clear correlation to task properties. Death risk doesn't concentrate at particular episode phases—it can happen whenever the agent attempts a physical state change—and the failure modes collapse into just one robust category: physical state transition hallucinations. Finer sub-classifications don't reach statistical significance.\n\nThe 2-way versus 3-way distinction also adds minimal value because the fast versus deep internal reasoning split rarely changes outcomes in ScienceWorld. Tasks where internal reasoning succeeds are simple enough that fast reasoning works fine, and tasks where it fails tend to fail regardless of depth. The 0.4-point gap with confidence intervals crossing zero suggests the router should just decide whether to simulate or not, which simplifies the method but also weakens the core claim about compute efficiency.\n\nThe v3 boil results confirm the death avoidance signal on physical state tasks, with the oracle score improving from -29.7 to -10.5 due to better trajectory-level label composition. The key finding remains: deterministic simulation provides roughly a 30-point advantage over internal reasoning on boil tasks through death prevention. All three router sizes perform within noise of each other, which aligns with the noisy labels limiting what any router can learn. The accuracy improvement from 56% to 60% as size increases from 1.5B to 3B is modest and doesn't translate to meaningful end-to-end differences, supporting the lightweight router claim but revealing the uncomfortable truth: label quality is the bottleneck, not router capacity.\n\nPlan-009 got deprioritized because ScienceWorld results were weak and selective routing performed about the same as always-deterministic, so running MATH would likely show a similar pattern where the code interpreter helps but learning when to invoke it adds little value. I'm redirecting resources to deeper analysis of the ScienceWorld data instead.\n\nCross-task transfer fails because the routing signal is fundamentally task-specific—the router learns \"this is a death-risk task\" rather than generalizable features about when simulation helps. Chemistry to physics transfer drops to 49% accuracy because surface-level features like mentions of heat don't transfer to physics tasks with electrical circuits and motion. This negative result undermines the claim that explicit routers learn generalizable routing but supports the pivot by showing routing value is highly task-dependent.\n\nNow I'm organizing everything into a clean YAML structure and deciding which tables to include for each experiment.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Now I'm verifying the marginal and low-risk group averages across all four tools, working through the calculations to ensure each category's mean is correct before moving forward.\n\nI'm noticing that SimGate underperforms compared to the internal baseline on low-risk tasks—it scores 81.3 versus 83.3—which suggests the noisy routing occasionally misdirects to the detector when the internal model would be