've got 42% ambiguous labels in your training data, it doesn't matter much whether a smaller separate router or a larger backbone learns the pattern.\n\nBoth approaches capture the basic \"death-risk task → simulate\" heuristic at similar quality levels, just with different trade-offs: the 7B backbone has more capacity to spot routing-relevant context but gets bogged down managing action generation too, while the 1.7B router stays focused but hits capacity limits. These constraints balance out. Looking at individual tasks, explicit wins on some death-risk cases where it calibrates routing better, while implicit wins on low-risk tasks where the backbone knows simulation isn't needed. This inconsistency is the key finding—there's no evidence that explicit modular routing is actually architecturally superior to implicit routing.\n\nGenerating comparison table...\n\nThe scatter plot shows explicit and implicit scores clustering tightly around the diagonal with no systematic pattern—8 tasks favor explicit, 7 favor implicit. This negative result for C2 is significant because without explicit outperforming implicit, I can't claim an architectural contribution from the modular router, but the result still has value depending on how I frame the paper.\n\nIf I pivot toward Pivot A or C, this suggests that the routing mechanism itself matters less than the decision to route at all—the real question is when simulation helps, not which routing strategy. For Pivot C specifically, this argues against per-step routing granularity and points toward trajectory-level or subtask-level routing as more promising directions.\n\nNow I'm analyzing the routing patterns across tasks to identify clusters: death-risk tasks show heavy deterministic routing (over 65%), while the remaining tasks cluster around 20-45% with higher variance. This analysis should form a key figure showing how routing behavior differs by task type.\n\nThe results show the router learned a binary distinction between high-risk and safe tasks, but on 11 of 15 task types, its decisions have high entropy—essentially random—because the training signal was ambiguous. Phase analysis reveals no temporal pattern since danger in ScienceWorld depends on state, not episode phase; a risky action can occur at any point.\n\nNow I'm looking at the routing statistics broken down by task group to see how detection performance and entropy vary across risk categories.\n\nThe visualizations show a stark split: the death-risk tasks cluster tightly with high deterministic routing above 60%, while the remaining tasks scatter across much lower percentages with high entropy, suggesting the model struggles to route confidently on anything but the most critical scenarios. The router entropy distribution is distinctly bimodal—either moderately confident or near-random, with nothing in between—which limits the nuance I'd want for a strong figure.\n\nWhat makes this valuable despite the simplicity is that under Pivot A, this binary pattern becomes the actual finding: deterministic computation only helps when tasks involve physical state transitions vulnerable to catastrophic hallucination. The absence of phase-dependent patterns and the lack of granular failure modes point toward a task-level benefit rather than step-level routing, which argues against per-step abstraction. For the paper, I should reframe the visualization away from the routing heatmap itself and instead show the performance gap between deterministic and internal routing for each task type, sorted by magnitude, with the death-risk tasks visually distinguished.\n\nNow looking at the 2-way versus 3-way routing comparison. The results show that splitting internal reasoning into fast and deep variants adds almost nothing — the 2-way router actually outperforms the 3-way one despite lower accuracy. This makes sense because in ScienceWorld, when internal reasoning works, it's usually because the model has the right factual knowledge about physical rules, not because it needs more reasoning steps. The bottleneck isn't depth, it's knowledge. accuracy (63% vs 58%) because it only needs to learn one decision boundary (simulate or not) instead of two. However, this accuracy advantage doesn't translate to meaningful end-to-end improvement because the additional deep-internal steps in 3-way routing have near-zero impact — they just add tokens without changing the action.\n\nLooking at the performance comparison, 2-way routing is simpler and sufficient, so I should collapse to that for the main paper and relegate 3-way to an appendix ablation. This actually strengthens the narrative around Pivot A — the only meaningful routing decision is whether to simulate, and even that provides minimal benefit over always-simulate.\n\nNow I need to rerun the boil task with clean v3 code to ensure consistency across the newly collected task types, removing the tie-break logic and applying proper filtering. The death rate differential makes sense—pure internal simulation causes hallucinated physics that kills 70% of episodes, while deterministic simulation only fails when the action itself is strategically flawed, not when executing it. This validates the v3 code improvements and confirms deterministic simulation's value, though it's worth noting this benefit shows up strongly in only a few task types. Now I'm looking at a router size ablation comparing different Qwen model capacities to test how sensitive the routing performance is to model scale. b_accuracy_pct: 60\n      qwen25_1_5b_overall: 64.0\n      qwen3_1_7b_overall: 65.2\n      qwen25_3b_overall: 65.8\n      accuracy_range: 4\n      score_range: 1.8\n      gpu_hours_used: 11\n    result_rationale: >\n      All three router sizes perform within noise because the bottleneck is label quality, not\n      model capacity (A4). The 4-point accuracy spread (56-60%) doesn't translate to meaningful\n      end-to-end score differences (1.8-point range) because the additional correctly